    "signalPanelInMixInputExplain": "Whether you hear this input at all. It has no effect on recording: a take prints the signal itself, not what you monitor. The three below decide WHEN this opens — muted here and nothing below can bring it back.",
    "@signalPanelInMixInputExplain": {
        "description": "The muted/heard pair on the INPUT card, where the generic 'still records and still plays' is meaningless — the monitor path never records and never plays. Answers the question the input card actually raises: why two switches for hearing one input. Both gates must be open (engine_process.c: `!mon_on[c] || mon_mut[c]` silences)."
    },
    "signalPanelLevelInputExplain": "How loud this input is in what you hear. Like the switches under it, it never reaches a take: a recording prints the signal itself, at the level it arrived.",
    "@signalPanelLevelInputExplain": {
        "description": "The gain row on the INPUT card. The generic string says 'this chain in the mix', but an input's fader is the monitor's own volume — engine mon_vol, applied only in mix_monitors_frame and never recorded. Same argument as the in-mix and chain splits."
    },
    "signalPanelChainInputExplain": "The effects this input passes through, in order. Switching the chain off leaves the effects loaded, takes them out of what you hear — and prints new takes dry, since a take copies the chain that was on when it started.",
    "@signalPanelChainInputExplain": {
        "description": "The chain caption on the INPUT card. The generic string stops at 'out of the sound', which on an input is half the story: _snapshotMonitorChainsOntoLanes bails on a disabled chain, so the take is dry too. Saying so only after the fact — in fxChainOffInputConsequence — tells people once it is too late."
    },
